climate change affects us all but around  the world will disproportionately impact  the lives of people of color  yet climate protests in the uk are seen  as not  racially diverse enough  extinction rebellion has forced the  climate debate with high-profile civil  disobedience resulting  in huge numbers of arrests but the risk  of  charges and fines has deterred some  lower-income protesters  and people of color so we're just going  to go and sit the band  lucy craig now aged 73 was arrested in  one of the first  major xr demonstrations and says she was  unafraid of the repercussions  it was really no skin off my back being  arrested but if you were a young  black male or female i don't think it  would be nearly as easy  you think that being a kind of  middle-class well-off  white woman helped you within the  protest  absolutely everything is fueled by white  privilege  um including activism yeah  yeah for sure however well-meaning  rosemary mallet says tackling climate  change can't just be driven  by white activists the archdeacon of  croydon has established herself  as a leading figure on climate change  within the church of england  trying to give a voice to those who feel  less empowered to bring about  change many black people understand that  if they are arrested  it's going to have a tremendous impact  on their lives  on perhaps their employment and so many  other ways in which they and their  family lives their lives  and she questions whether the climate  movement is at a crossroads  i think there is a need for an evolution  away from  the kind of sharp shocking protest i  think we've we've done it  and i think with any group it needs to  evolve and think about its tactics  in how it wants to change the debate  this group of climate activists who  formed the air pollution group choked up  snapped themselves talking on zoom they  didn't want the death of ella kisi  deborah who died from air pollution in  2013 to be in vain  ella lived by the busy south circular  road in south london  her friend anjali ramon middleton says  all environmental activism in the uk  is seen as a very white activity  it is more difficult to be a climate  activist  if you are a person of color and i think  a lot of this is about the way that  environment and  environmental issues are framed by um  the movement and they're often framed  through a very  um white middle-class perspective  lockdown has brought about an enforced  shutdown of some  forms of climate activism an opportunity  to regroup  or perhaps reboot the movement lisa  holland  sky news
 

